  "worked_solution": "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-3,$ $y=1,$ and $y=4.$\n\n- We're given that $y=4$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=4$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=1$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=1$ (already drawn by the above property) and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-3$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=-3$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just above $y=-3$ points downward, the arrow just below it points downward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/e428cf67a486e9eb17764727ab042bb44e07dfe4f13c125112fb56799d5aadea.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that corresponds with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/26f65c17a5b108cc0c739bbfa5d7971841bfe71dc8886fb801e2ca5b5d2a32ae.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-3,$ $y=1,$ and $y=4.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-3$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking below the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking above the line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=1$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=4$ appear to converge toward this line.",
